thread:
samuel: Hey team - customer on prod seeing a spike in 499/aborted requests and duplicate downstream billing. They use our Node TS SDK 0.8.1 with Node 18.16.0. Stack shows AbortError coming from undici. Any ideas?

natalie: Can you paste the minimal request + how they instantiate the SDK and timeouts?

samuel: Sure. They wrap fetch with AbortController and also enabled SDK retries/backoff (default). Minimal snippet they run:```ts
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY, retry: { retries: 2, backoffMs: 200 } });
const ac = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 3000);
await client.text.generate({ model: 'gpt-ogg-small', input: 'hello' , signal: ac.signal });
```
They reported that when requests time out the SDK sometimes sends the same payload twice (customer sees 2 token charges) and logs show undici: "request aborted" then another retry goes through.

ben: This reads like a mismatch between the built-in global fetch (node18 undici) and an installed undici package. If the SDK triggers an internal retry after it thinks the previous request failed but the AbortSignal already aborted or wasn't forwarded properly, you can get a replay. What versions are in package-lock?

samuel: package.json:
```json
{"dependencies": {"@redwood/inference": "0.8.1", "undici": "5.12.0"}}
```
node -v = v18.16.0

devx: Yep, that's the likely culprit. Node 18 has a built-in undici-based fetch, but installing undici can create two competing implementations. Our SDK expects to control retries and to wire the AbortSignal to the underlying request. If the shipped undici version differs, AbortSignal semantics might differ across versions.

natalie: Repro steps request from support: can you run this locally and paste results?

samuel: Repro script (very small):```js
// repro.js
const { RedwoodClient } = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY, retry: { retries: 2, backoffMs: 200 } });
const ac = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 1000);
(async () => {
  try {
    await client.text.generate({ model: 'gpt-ogg-small', input: 'repeat test', signal: ac.signal });
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('err', e && e.name, e && e.message);
  }
})();
```
Run with: `NODE_OPTIONS=--no-warnings node repro.js`

jamie: Quick checks: 1) Does the SDK have an option to disable automatic retries? 2) Try removing the undici dependency so Node uses built-in fetch. 3) Or pin undici to the same version the SDK expects.

ben: Also check whether the SDK uses `fetch` polyfill or undici.requestPool. If it uses its own retry loop and then calls fetch again while an AbortSignal is in a terminal state, that can cause immediate aborted errors while a subsequent attempt still goes out. We saw this before when using AbortController + axios retries.

devx: Suggested immediate mitigations for the customer: set retry.retries = 0 in SDK config, or avoid passing the external AbortSignal into SDK and instead use timeout on the SDK call (e.g., SDK-level timeout option) so retries are aware.

samuel: Tried disabling retries (retries:0) locally — single abort observed, no duplicate request. With retries enabled + undici package present, we saw double attempts in tcpdump.

natalie: Can you confirm which SDK internals call site forwards the signal? @jamie any pointer to the code?

jamie: The client code attaches the signal when calling `this._transport.request(...)`. If the transport is using an external undici instance that expects AbortSignal.cancel vs AbortController.abort semantics, it can behave differently. I'll open a quick PR to 1) add defensive check: if signal.aborted then throw immediately, 2) avoid replaying the same body unless `safeRetries` is enabled.

ops-bot: deploy-bot: New canary build of SDK 0.8.2 published to npm (ci/1234).

ben: Long-term fix: standardize on global fetch where possible, and add instrumentation to mark idempotent vs non-idempotent requests. We should also add a prominent note in the TS SDK docs about using external AbortController + retries.

samuel: For support ticket — recommended steps for customer: 1) Set SDK retry.retries=0 or use safeRetries flag; 2) Remove explicit `undici` from deps (let Node's global fetch win) or pin undici to the version the SDK vendor-tests against; 3) Upgrade to SDK 0.8.2 once the canary verifies fix; 4) If they need abort semantics + retries, use SDK timeout option rather than passing external AbortSignal.

natalie: I'll send this guidance and repro snippet to the customer and escalate to eng if they can't upgrade. Thanks. :thumbsup:

jamie: Filed GH issue linking this thread and will follow up with PR and tests (gh#4521).

samuel: Thanks all, will update ticket with repro results and snapshot of tcpdump evidence.
